From: PS3 Custom Toolchain
Subject: [PATCH] cell64lv2: raise the aligned-new threshold to the target biggest alignment (16 bytes)

The ordinary _mallocr on this target already returns 16-byte-aligned
blocks: newlib patch 0015 sets _mallocr's MALLOC_ALIGNMENT to 16, and
the size/split math carries that alignment -- chunk2mem is aligned
when the heap is acquired, and allocated-and-split sizes are rounded,
realloc included.  (vec_malloc is a separate API, _memalign_r(...,16,
...), and vec_mallocr's MINSIZE is sizeof(struct malloc_chunk), 32
under LP64, not a fixed 16; neither is the guarantee.)
rs6000.h nonetheless hardcodes MALLOC_ABI_ALIGNMENT to 64, a
conservative word-sized default shared across the PowerPC family.  That
value feeds both the C++ aligned-new threshold, which the compiler
derives as

    aligned_new_threshold = malloc_alignment() / BITS_PER_UNIT
      = MAX(max_align_t_align(), MALLOC_ABI_ALIGNMENT) / BITS_PER_UNIT,

and the compiler's model of what malloc returns; with the threshold at
8 bytes every 'new' of a type with a 16-byte-aligned member -- anything
holding a vector, or a 16-byte-aligned field -- called the aligned
operator new, even though the ordinary allocator already served a
16-byte-aligned block.

Raise the promise in the target header (last in the tm_file chain, so
this define overrides the 64 in rs6000.h and the BITS_PER_WORD default
in defaults.h).  128 is the target's BIGGEST_ALIGNMENT expressed in
bits, and it is the exact value the aligned-new dispatch and the
__STDCPP_DEFAULT_NEW_ALIGNMENT__ predefine use in both data models.

A custom malloc replacement must also return 16-byte-aligned blocks:
the compiler now assumes 16-byte alignment for malloc, calloc, realloc,
strdup and strndup.

Behaviour change for code that overloaded the aligned operator new and
relied on 16-byte-aligned types reaching it: every such type now
resolves to plain operator new.

--- a/gcc/config/rs6000/cell64lv2.h
+++ b/gcc/config/rs6000/cell64lv2.h
@@ -562,3 +562,18 @@
 /* IEEE 128-bit emulator type handling.  */
 #undef	TARGET_FLOAT128_ENABLE_TYPE
 #define TARGET_FLOAT128_ENABLE_TYPE 1
+
+/* The ordinary _mallocr already returns 16-byte-aligned blocks
+   (newlib patch 0015 sets _mallocr's MALLOC_ALIGNMENT to 16, and the
+   size/split math carries that alignment; realloc included).
+   rs6000.h nonetheless fixes MALLOC_ABI_ALIGNMENT at 64 bits, which
+   sets both the C++ aligned-new threshold
+   ( MAX(max_align_t_align(), MALLOC_ABI_ALIGNMENT) / 8 ) and the
+   compiler's model of malloc's guarantee; at 8 every 'new' of a type
+   with a 16-byte-aligned member called the aligned operator new.
+   Raise the promise to the target's biggest alignment (128 bits) so
+   plain operator new serves those types.  A custom malloc replacement
+   must also return 16-byte-aligned blocks, because the compiler now
+   assumes it for malloc/calloc/realloc/strdup/strndup.  */
+#undef MALLOC_ABI_ALIGNMENT
+#define MALLOC_ABI_ALIGNMENT 128
